WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.350
Some counting problems are too awkward to do in one go.

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:05.430
The trick is to split them into cases.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:09.180
Good cases have two properties: no outcome belongs to

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:11.910
two cases, and every outcome belongs to one.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.600
Multiplying choices only works when the number of

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.110
choices does not depend on what you chose earlier.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:20.510
When a restriction changes the choices,

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:22.750
a single multiplication breaks.

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:25.370
Splitting at the restriction repairs it.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.470
Using the digits zero, one, two, three and four,

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:32.480
with no digit repeated, how many even three-digit

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:33.630
numbers can be made?

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:36.550
Pause and think about which place causes the trouble.

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:43.400
The number must be even, so the last digit is zero,

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:44.640
two or four.

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:47.450
But zero is special, because a number cannot

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:48.580
start with zero.

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:52.730
So split by the last digit: either it is zero, or it is

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:53.580
two or four.

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.350
If the last digit is zero, the first digit has four

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:00.460
choices and the middle has three: twelve numbers.

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:04.690
If the last digit is two or four, the first digit cannot

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:07.810
be zero or the last digit, leaving three choices,

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:09.520
and the middle has three.

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:13.080
That is two times three times three: eighteen numbers.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.040
The cases cannot overlap, because a number has only

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.110
one last digit.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:20.870
Add them: twelve plus eighteen is thirty.

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:24.340
Sometimes it is quicker to count what is forbidden.

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:26.980
Two switches have four settings altogether.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.240
If "both on" is not allowed,

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:31.220
subtract that one, leaving three.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:35.090
Counting everything and subtracting the unwanted part is

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:36.240
called using the complement.

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:39.380
Split at the feature that changes the choices.

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:41.400
Multiply within a case.

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:42.680
Add the cases.

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:45.930
Notebook challenge: how many two-digit numbers have

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:46.920
two different digits?
